    thinking: "**Defining Game Modes**\n\nI\'m currently fleshing out the design for a 3-mode game architecture, specifically the `sandbox`, `editor`, and `match` modes. I\'m focusing on the precise requirements for each mode, particularly the `PlayMode` type and its implications for user interaction within the `/src/game/FactoryPlaygroundCanvas.tsx` component. I\'m carefully considering how these modes will interact and how to handle mode transitions.\n\n\n**Enumerating Editor Tools**\n\nI\'ve defined `PlayMode` as a union of \'sandbox\', \'editor\', and \'match\', and also defined `EditorToolId` to represent various assets available in the editor. I\'ve now expanded the `EditorToolId` to cover 7 map editor categories, creating unique identifiers for each. These include purple and gold trees, a turbine, a power core, concrete fences and barricades, explosive barrels and hydrants, and options for moving the HQ and other important positions.\n\n\n**Defining Map Layouts**\n\nI\'ve just solidified the structure for saving and loading arena map layouts. I\'ve created the `ArenaMapLayout` and `PlacedMapProp` interfaces for serializable data in `localStorage`. I\'m now working on the default layout, and I\'m ensuring all requested elements from the user, including specific tree types, are properly included. Next, I\'ll focus on how to integrate the movement of key locations like HQ and drop zones.\n\n\n**Populating Default Arena**\n\nI\'m now populating the default arena map layout. I\'ve placed several `tree_purple` and `tree_gold` trees near the player\'s starting point and along the arena. I\'ve also added default props such as a `power_core`, `turbine`, `concrete_fence`, `barricade`, `explosive_barrel`, and `hydrant`. These are all added as props that can be interacted with, including destructible trees which are added to the `trees` array.\n\n\n**Analyzing Play Mode Logic**\n\nI\'m now diving into the implications of `playMode` on NPC spawning and AI behavior within the `FactoryPlaygroundCanvas.tsx` component. In sandbox mode, only the player unit is present, and AI loops are disabled. When transitioning back to \'sandbox\' or \'editor\' from \'match\', non-player units and projectiles are removed, and base health is reset. Editor mode triggers a top-down camera view, essential for map design purposes.\n\n\n**Implementing Editor Movement**\n\nI\'ve added camera controls specifically for editor mode. Now, WASD or arrow keys and middle/right-click dragging will smoothly pan the editor view using `editorPan.x` and `editorPan.z`. Mouse scroll zooms the view, and the 3D editor cursor ring with axis indicator is implemented, showing placement points, rotation, and scale when the editor is active. I also added click logic to move hq and drop zone locations.\n\n\n**Refining Editor Interaction**\n\nI\'ve implemented the logic for various editor tools. Moving the red pad and drop zone now updates their respective positions and data. Other tools spawn assets at specified locations, and I\'m using right-click to delete props within range. I\'ve also integrated keyboard shortcuts for rotating the editor\'s rotation and placed objects in the scene.\n\n\n**Expanding Match Mode**\n\nI\'ve implemented the game\'s match mode which instantly spawns AI-controlled allies and enemies, activating full AI behavior for crate interaction and base combat. My focus is on ensuring the AI responds properly to the environment. I\'m also clamping the editor scale and added scaling logic to props and trees. Now, bracket keys dynamically change prop sizes, a vital feature for editing maps.\n\n\n**Testing Sandbox Enhancements**\n\nI\'m now implementing new sandbox UI shortcuts for rapid testing. I\'m adding buttons to instantly drop red and blue crates, and to spawn an Lyo Tank in front of me. I\'m also preparing to test the Lyo Tank\'s assembly, weapon attachment, and vertical movement controls. My aim is to streamline the testing workflow, removing time spent waiting for crates or manually spawning. I\'m also ensuring the game starts in \'playing\' mode unless the title screen is requested via a URL parameter.\n\n\n"
