  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Camera Requirements**\n\nI\'m currently focused on dissecting the user\'s requirements for the low-angle horizon chase camera. The critical point is avoiding a top-down view that only shows the ground; the goal is to capture the sky and tree canopies. This emphasis on a specific viewing angle is the core of the problem.\n\n\n**Verifying Camera Angles**\n\nI\'ve specified the camera\'s key parameters: **Y = +5.2**, **Z = +12.5**, and **FOV = 42\302\260**, for that cinematic view. The `camera.lookAt` point is set to `targetPos.y + 2.4`, tilting towards the mouse cursor. I\'m focusing on ensuring 25-30% of the upper screen shows the horizon and canopy. I will check for any conflicts with the test, and update the UI label.\n\n\n**Refining Camera Positioning**\n\nI\'m now refining the camera positioning logic within `/src/game/FactoryPlaygroundCanvas.tsx`.  The default `cameraZoom` is set to 1.0, and `targetPos` now varies by play mode: using the tank in sandbox/match, the player unit, and a specific point for the title screen. The editor mode will maintain the overhead view for easier layout tooling, while the rest are using the new calculations based on follow positioning, mouse pan, recoil, and shake.\n\n\n**Calculating Camera Angles**\n\nI\'m now calculating the precise pitch angle. I\'ve determined the camera position using `camY`, `camZ`, along with `fovScale`, `mousePanCurrent`, `recoilOffset`, and `shakeZ`. Now, I\'m focusing on defining `camera.lookAt`. The target is calculated as `targetPos.y + 2.4` with a tilt towards the mouse cursor. I am now trying to quantify the exact vertical angle between `camera.position` and `camera.lookAt`, to ensure the horizon and canopy appear correctly on screen.\n\n\n**Quantifying Visual Horizon**\n\nI\'ve just calculated the screen projection of the horizon.  At infinite distance, the horizon should appear around 20.8% from the top. At a distance of 55 units, the ground level is 5.4 degrees below the horizontal. This reveals how the ground projection varies with distance, which will impact camera positioning and FOV decisions.\n\n\n**Verifying Horizon Projection**\n\nI\'ve just validated my horizon projection calculations with code. With the camera set as described, the horizon line naturally projects at 26-30% from the top of the screen at a distance of 65-75 units. This aligns well with the existing ground geometry in the simulation, confirming that my camera angle strategy effectively captures the desired visual horizon and canopy composition. I\'m now fine-tuning the camera positioning logic.\n\n\n**Confirming Visual Projection**\n\nI\'m now confirming the visual projection with the camera. With the warm evening sky and purple & golden Lyo tree canopies, the top 25-30% of the screen should display both. At a height of 5.2, the trees\' height of 4.5 to 9.5 literally rises above the horizon into the warm evening sky. I will now check blue HQ occlusion when camera is at (0, 5.2, 25).\n\n\n**Detecting Obstruction Issues**\n\nI\'ve just found a potential obstruction problem. The calculated camera view at (0, 5.2, 25) to (0, 2.4, 12.5) has a direct line of sight through `z = 22.0`, where the `Blue HQ` is situated!  I\'m now checking the `DEFAULT_ARENA_MAP_LAYOUT` for `blueHq` and `redHq` positions to migrate the stored layouts. I will update the map to reposition them.\n\n\n**Visualizing Vista Composition**\n\nI can now visualize the composition of the vista. From (0, 5.2, 25.0), the road at x=0 is completely open, providing an unobstructed view. Tall swaying grass fills the bottom, the player or tank in the midground, flanked by structures. The background features purple and gold Lyo trees, their canopies rising into the evening sky and forming an appealing horizon. I will add a few purple trees to frame the central view.\n\n\n**Constructing the Sky Dome**\n\nI\'m now focused on generating a more realistic warm evening sky. Currently, the code uses a flat background color, which doesn\'t convey the desired visual. I am building a cel-shaded sky dome with stylized horizon silhouettes that move with the camera. I\'m focusing on the shader material for this large sphere to achieve that warm evening sky effect.\n\n\n**Crafting the Sky Dome**\n\nI\'m now implementing the sky dome shader. I\'m aiming for a cel-shaded effect with specific color gradients: golden-peach near the horizon, transitioning to lavender-rose, then deep twilight periwinkle-indigo. I\'m also adding a stylized setting sun and comic clouds. The distant fog will match the horizon glow to blend seamlessly.\n\n\n**Adjusting Ground Detail**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the foreground detail. I\'ve been reviewing the code for the dirt road and grass. I\'m increasing the grass blade dimensions and scaling to create a lusher foreground in the camera\'s view, which will appear much more prominently in the low-angle shot. The dirt road helps by framing the shot.\n\n\n"
